#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561297 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{8}} \\int_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}} r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{8}} \\int_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}} r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta & = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{8}} \\left[\\frac{r^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{8}} \\left(\\frac{\\left(4 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\right)^{2}-\\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{8}} 8 \\cos^{2}{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the double-angle formula\n\n$$\n\\cos(2x)=2\\cos^2 x-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2\\theta$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\cos^2\\left(2\\theta\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(1+\\cos\\left(4\\theta\\right)\\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we can finish our integral by integrating with respect to $\\theta,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{8}} 8 \\cos^{2}{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta & = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{8}} 4\\left(1+\\cos\\left(4\\theta\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = 4\\left[\\theta + \\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 \\theta \\right)}}{4}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{8}} \\\\\n& = 4\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{8}+\\frac{1}{4}\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(0+\\frac{1}{4}\\sin\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\\n& = 4\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{8}+\\frac{1}{4} \\left(1\\right)\\right)-\\left(0+\\frac{1}{4} \\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\\n& = 4\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{8}\\right)+\\frac{1}{4}\\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\\n& = 1 + \\frac{\\pi}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561303 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\int_{0}^{\\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}} 2 r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\int_{0}^{\\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}} 2 r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta & = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} 2 \\int_{0}^{\\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}} r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} 2 \\left[\\frac{r^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{\\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} 2 \\left(\\frac{\\left(\\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\right)^{2}-\\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the double-angle formula\n\n$$\n\\cos(2x)=1-2\\sin^2 x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2\\theta$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\sin^2\\left(2\\theta\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(1-\\cos\\left(4\\theta\\right)\\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we can finish our integral by integrating with respect to $\\theta,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta & = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\frac{1}{2}\\left(1-\\cos\\left(4\\theta\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = \\frac{1}{2}\\left[\\theta - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 \\theta \\right)}}{4}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\\\\n& = \\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}-\\frac{1}{4}\\sin\\left(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right)\\right)-\\left(0-\\frac{1}{4}\\sin\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\\n& = \\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}-\\frac{1}{4} \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(0-\\frac{1}{4} \\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\\n& = \\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)-\\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n& = - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{16} + \\frac{\\pi}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561304 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\int_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}} 3 r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\int_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}} 3 r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta & = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} 3 \\int_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}} r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} 3 \\left[\\frac{r^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} 3 \\left(\\frac{\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\right)^{2}-\\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} 6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the double-angle formula\n\n$$\n\\cos(2x)=2\\cos^2 x-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2\\theta$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\cos^2\\left(2\\theta\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(1+\\cos\\left(4\\theta\\right)\\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we can finish our integral by integrating with respect to $\\theta,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} 6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta & = \\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} 3\\left(1+\\cos\\left(4\\theta\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm d \\theta \\\\\n& = 3\\left[\\theta + \\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 \\theta \\right)}}{4}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\\\\n& = 3\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}+\\frac{1}{4}\\sin\\left(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right)\\right)-\\left(0+\\frac{1}{4}\\sin\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\\n& = 3\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}+\\frac{1}{4} \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(0+\\frac{1}{4} \\left(0\\right)\\right)\\right) \\\\\n& = 3\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)+\\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n& = \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{8} + \\frac{\\pi}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561317 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x e^{x y},$ $x=(t - 1)^{2}$ and $y=(t - 3)^{2},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=x y e^{x y} + e^{x y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x^{2} e^{x y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2} e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}} + e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(t - 1)^{4} e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} &=2 t - 2,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=2 t - 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =((t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2} e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}} + e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}})\\cdot (2 t - 2)+((t - 1)^{4} e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}})\\cdot (2 t - 6) \\\\  & =(2 t - 2) e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}} + ((t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2} (2 t - 2) e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}}) + ((t - 1)^{4} (2 t - 6) e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}}) \\\\  & =2 (t - 1) ((t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2} + (t - 3) (t - 1)^{3} + 1) e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561328 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x e^{x y},$ $x=3 t^{4}$ and $y=t^{3},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=x y e^{x y} + e^{x y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x^{2} e^{x y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =3 t^{7} e^{3 t^{7}} + e^{3 t^{7}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =9 t^{8} e^{3 t^{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} &=12 t^{3},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=3 t^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =(3 t^{7} e^{3 t^{7}} + e^{3 t^{7}})\\cdot (12 t^{3})+(9 t^{8} e^{3 t^{7}})\\cdot (3 t^{2}) \\\\  & =12 t^{3} e^{3 t^{7}} + (36 t^{10} e^{3 t^{7}}) + (27 t^{10} e^{3 t^{7}}) \\\\  & =3 t^{3} (21 t^{7} + 4) e^{3 t^{7}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561337 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x e^{x y},$ $x=t^{3}$ and $y=t^{2},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=x y e^{x y} + e^{x y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x^{2} e^{x y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =t^{5} e^{t^{5}} + e^{t^{5}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =t^{6} e^{t^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} &=3 t^{2},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=2 t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =(t^{5} e^{t^{5}} + e^{t^{5}})\\cdot (3 t^{2})+(t^{6} e^{t^{5}})\\cdot (2 t) \\\\  & =3 t^{2} e^{t^{5}} + (3 t^{7} e^{t^{5}}) + (2 t^{7} e^{t^{5}}) \\\\  & =t^{2} (5 t^{5} + 3) e^{t^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561348 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x e^{x y},$ $x=(3 t + 2)^{2}$ and $y=(3 t + 2)^{2},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=x y e^{x y} + e^{x y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x^{2} e^{x y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(3 t + 2)^{4} e^{(3 t + 2)^{4}} + e^{(3 t + 2)^{4}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(3 t + 2)^{4} e^{(3 t + 2)^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} &=18 t + 12,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=18 t + 12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =((3 t + 2)^{4} e^{(3 t + 2)^{4}} + e^{(3 t + 2)^{4}})\\cdot (18 t + 12)+((3 t + 2)^{4} e^{(3 t + 2)^{4}})\\cdot (18 t + 12) \\\\  & =(18 t + 12) e^{(3 t + 2)^{4}} + ((3 t + 2)^{4} (18 t + 12) e^{(3 t + 2)^{4}}) + ((3 t + 2)^{4} (18 t + 12) e^{(3 t + 2)^{4}}) \\\\  & =6 (3 t + 2) (2 (3 t + 2)^{4} + 1) e^{(3 t + 2)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561352 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x e^{x y},$ $x=2 t^{2}$ and $y=t^{3},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=x y e^{x y} + e^{x y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x^{2} e^{x y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2 t^{5} e^{2 t^{5}} + e^{2 t^{5}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =4 t^{4} e^{2 t^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} &=4 t,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=3 t^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =(2 t^{5} e^{2 t^{5}} + e^{2 t^{5}})\\cdot (4 t)+(4 t^{4} e^{2 t^{5}})\\cdot (3 t^{2}) \\\\  & =4 t e^{2 t^{5}} + (8 t^{6} e^{2 t^{5}}) + (12 t^{6} e^{2 t^{5}}) \\\\  & =4 t (5 t^{5} + 1) e^{2 t^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561355 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x e^{x y},$ $x=(3 t + 2)^{2}$ and $y=(3 t - 2)^{2},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=x y e^{x y} + e^{x y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x^{2} e^{x y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(3 t - 2)^{2} (3 t + 2)^{2} e^{(3 t - 2)^{2} (3 t + 2)^{2}} + e^{(3 t - 2)^{2} (3 t + 2)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(3 t + 2)^{4} e^{(3 t - 2)^{2} (3 t + 2)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} &=18 t + 12,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=18 t - 12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =((3 t - 2)^{2} (3 t + 2)^{2} e^{(3 t - 2)^{2} (3 t + 2)^{2}} + e^{(3 t - 2)^{2} (3 t + 2)^{2}})\\cdot (18 t + 12)+((3 t + 2)^{4} e^{(3 t - 2)^{2} (3 t + 2)^{2}})\\cdot (18 t - 12) \\\\  & =(18 t + 12) e^{(3 t - 2)^{2} (3 t + 2)^{2}} + ((3 t - 2)^{2} (3 t + 2)^{2} (18 t + 12) e^{(3 t - 2)^{2} (3 t + 2)^{2}}) + ((3 t + 2)^{4} (18 t - 12) e^{(3 t - 2)^{2} (3 t + 2)^{2}}) \\\\  & =6 (3 t + 2) ((3 t - 2)^{2} (3 t + 2)^{2} + (3 t - 2) (3 t + 2)^{3} + 1) e^{(3 t - 2)^{2} (3 t + 2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561364 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x e^{x y},$ $x=4 t^{2}$ and $y=t^{4},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=x y e^{x y} + e^{x y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x^{2} e^{x y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =4 t^{6} e^{4 t^{6}} + e^{4 t^{6}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =16 t^{4} e^{4 t^{6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} &=8 t,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=4 t^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =(4 t^{6} e^{4 t^{6}} + e^{4 t^{6}})\\cdot (8 t)+(16 t^{4} e^{4 t^{6}})\\cdot (4 t^{3}) \\\\  & =8 t e^{4 t^{6}} + (32 t^{7} e^{4 t^{6}}) + (64 t^{7} e^{4 t^{6}}) \\\\  & =8 t (12 t^{6} + 1) e^{4 t^{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561366 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x e^{x y},$ $x=t^{2}$ and $y=t^{3},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=x y e^{x y} + e^{x y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x^{2} e^{x y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =t^{5} e^{t^{5}} + e^{t^{5}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =t^{4} e^{t^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} &=2 t,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=3 t^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =(t^{5} e^{t^{5}} + e^{t^{5}})\\cdot (2 t)+(t^{4} e^{t^{5}})\\cdot (3 t^{2}) \\\\  & =2 t e^{t^{5}} + (2 t^{6} e^{t^{5}}) + (3 t^{6} e^{t^{5}}) \\\\  & =t (5 t^{5} + 2) e^{t^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561368 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x e^{x y},$ $x=(t - 3)^{2}$ and $y=(t - 1)^{2},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=x y e^{x y} + e^{x y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x^{2} e^{x y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2} e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}} + e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(t - 3)^{4} e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} &=2 t - 6,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=2 t - 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =((t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2} e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}} + e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}})\\cdot (2 t - 6)+((t - 3)^{4} e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}})\\cdot (2 t - 2) \\\\  & =(2 t - 6) e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}} + ((t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2} (2 t - 6) e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}}) + ((t - 3)^{4} (2 t - 2) e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}}) \\\\  & =2 (t - 3) ((t - 3)^{3} (t - 1) + (t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2} + 1) e^{(t - 3)^{2} (t - 1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561373 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x e^{x y},$ $x=(2 t - 1)^{3}$ and $y=(2 t - 2)^{2},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=x y e^{x y} + e^{x y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x^{2} e^{x y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3} e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}} + e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(2 t - 1)^{6} e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} &=6 (2 t - 1)^{2},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=8 t - 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =((2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3} e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}} + e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}})\\cdot (6 (2 t - 1)^{2})+((2 t - 1)^{6} e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}})\\cdot (8 t - 8) \\\\  & =6 (2 t - 1)^{2} e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}} + (6 (2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{5} e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}}) + ((2 t - 1)^{6} (8 t - 8) e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}}) \\\\  & =2 (2 t - 1)^{2} (12 (t - 1)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3} + 4 (t - 1) (2 t - 1)^{4} + 3) e^{(2 t - 2)^{2} (2 t - 1)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561383 :question/problem "![](images/c0/c08b526332eb46874a5e2e9b171b3b2ad8d87dcff820a8fcf5508f8f4c5cb3d9.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $52{\\text{ km}}^{2}.$ What is the area of $△BCD$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\cdot 16\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 160\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘} \\\\ & ≈80{\\text{ km}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\ & =80-52 \\\\ & =28{\\text{ km}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561390 :question/problem "![](images/52/522af36614de497b371637bf138d0c81bd9588fcbad28d752042a5b1657cb9c4.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $62{\\text{ in}}^{2}.$ What is the area of $△BCD$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\cdot 18\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 180\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘} \\\\ & ≈90{\\text{ in}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\ & =90-62 \\\\ & =28{\\text{ in}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561393 :question/problem "![](images/b6/b62eaf4f8442f49bd6cb1deb47a3ed297f36cdbe30e25a7afa3b39bef9ef6b37.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $26{\\text{ mm}}^{2}.$ The area of the enclosing $△ABD$ is $47{\\text{ mm}}^{2}.$ What is the area of $△BCD$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "The difference of the areas of $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\ & =47-26 \\\\ & =21{\\text{ mm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561404 :question/problem "![](images/e8/e8c7278384f1710f1efd5cf7595b33271766e2dba3c14396c79a3f0e8fd0d7b2.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $34{\\text{ mm}}^{2}.$ The area of the enclosing $△ABD$ is $61{\\text{ mm}}^{2}.$ What is the area of $△BCD$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "The difference of the areas of $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\ & =61-34 \\\\ & =27{\\text{ mm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561408 :question/problem "![](images/69/69e2470ec4ccecef57b4dae5c8609b7d47accc884ce299cab84c825773a1d167.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $27{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$ The area of the enclosing $△ABD$ is $47{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$ What is the area of $△BCD$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "The difference of the areas of $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\ & =47-27 \\\\ & =20{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561409 :question/problem "![](images/3a/3a8c5071b79f521f06092b23b4f40e5a423848c5eca1c529fc783df00046e992.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $60{\\text{ in}}^{2}.$ The area of the enclosing $△ABD$ is $101{\\text{ in}}^{2}.$ What is the area of $△BCD$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "The difference of the areas of $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\ & =101-60 \\\\ & =41{\\text{ in}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561416 :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c11b42ac5b93fd75426cc649b6347344478c11a05ef80ab935928adfd75ce73.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $91{\\text{ km}}^{2}.$ The area of the enclosing $△ABD$ is $109{\\text{ km}}^{2}.$ What is the area of $△BCD$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "The difference of the areas of $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\ & =109-91 \\\\ & =18{\\text{ km}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561419 :question/problem "![](images/5c/5c90b8329b7c163cf4aff55af1bd45d21bc8487fcbf39c4b7b625b31d19bbb57.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $102{\\text{ mi}}^{2}.$ What is the area of $△BCD$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 16\\cdot 18\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 288\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘} \\\\ & ≈144{\\text{ mi}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\ & =144-102 \\\\ & =42{\\text{ mi}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561428 :question/problem "![](images/13/13ec62338798667cc6d164a32cb8dd4c0f6596eb7c3a6e0519cc492e80225877.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $25{\\text{ mm}}^{2}.$ What is the area of $△BCD$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 12\\cdot 8\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 96\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘} \\\\ & ≈48{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\ & =48-25 \\\\ & =23{\\text{ mm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561437 :question/problem "![](images/06/06d308a50c664795dd52ae3be40da7a0c23882fb11ba1d0f8f5d9d29bf1331a1.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $61{\\text{ mm}}^{2}.$ What is the area of $△BCD$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 16\\cdot 10\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 160\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘} \\\\ & ≈80{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\ & =80-61 \\\\ & =19{\\text{ mm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561455 :question/problem "![](images/f2/f2aba916c2a8e1513a9d1f79bbb6820559e188d3166ba9d4323cce018491f74d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(3,4)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=\\frac{7}{4}.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =\\left(\\frac{7}{4}\\right)^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =\\frac{49}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561460 :question/problem "![](images/18/1893e7f4fa7b6b3927a3b3750f526a747b4be615f91a60a446231df0b4bf8a91.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(2,3)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=\\frac{14}{3}.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =\\left(\\frac{14}{3}\\right)^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =\\frac{196}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561465 :question/problem "![](images/8d/8da1de86395af2b85313ba5fafe11d46aac395451f06a5820726eeabf71b8029.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(\\frac{17}{4},- \\frac{23}{4})$ and the radius of the circle is $r=\\frac{9}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-\\frac{17}{4})^{2}+(y-\\left(- \\frac{23}{4}\\right))^{2} & =\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)^{2} \\\\ (x-\\frac{17}{4})^{2}+(y+\\frac{23}{4})^{2} & =\\frac{81}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561479 :question/problem "![](images/c0/c00ac372925b7809ec02906f864ebd838b1fcf2b1a47c65edd9d70da296e847f.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(\\frac{9}{4},- \\frac{13}{3})$ and the radius of the circle is $r=\\frac{8}{3}.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-\\frac{9}{4})^{2}+(y-\\left(- \\frac{13}{3}\\right))^{2} & =\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)^{2} \\\\ (x-\\frac{9}{4})^{2}+(y+\\frac{13}{3})^{2} & =\\frac{64}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561480 :question/problem "![](images/7e/7e29e04a1ffb6494505d13590f004980c1b72cc508f40a4101b0033aed549114.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(-6,-3)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=\\frac{7}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-6))^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} & =\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)^{2} \\\\ (x+6)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{49}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561483 :question/problem "![](images/67/67b6e1a920ff90c43193ad005c570d8616c4efdf97f3f18fb327a64fe6371392.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(1,-5)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=\\frac{19}{4}.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-(-5))^{2} & =\\left(\\frac{19}{4}\\right)^{2} \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y+5)^{2} & =\\frac{361}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561490 :question/problem "![](images/bc/bc1921d801529e68bca5f4c8b8457eb98a167e5a3025e4b98e5d52ba3aef7061.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(\\frac{7}{3},- \\frac{11}{2})$ and the radius of the circle is $r=\\frac{4}{3}.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-\\frac{7}{3})^{2}+(y-\\left(- \\frac{11}{2}\\right))^{2} & =\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^{2} \\\\ (x-\\frac{7}{3})^{2}+(y+\\frac{11}{2})^{2} & =\\frac{16}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561507 :question/problem "![](images/b9/b9c17e0632b04ddaa61e1fcf25f9ed379d7e310f7ee271b2dc1a151e9a225748.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(\\frac{9}{2},- \\frac{21}{4})$ and the radius of the circle is $r=\\frac{19}{4}.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-\\frac{9}{2})^{2}+(y-\\left(- \\frac{21}{4}\\right))^{2} & =\\left(\\frac{19}{4}\\right)^{2} \\\\ (x-\\frac{9}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{21}{4})^{2} & =\\frac{361}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561508 :question/problem "![](images/89/89028234a6c156daa99692fe091bcbcb9eaefbe87ed34a4144eac333574763a1.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(- \\frac{23}{4},\\frac{23}{4})$ and the radius of the circle is $r=\\frac{7}{3}.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-\\left(- \\frac{23}{4}\\right))^{2}+(y-\\frac{23}{4})^{2} & =\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)^{2} \\\\ (x+\\frac{23}{4})^{2}+(y-\\frac{23}{4})^{2} & =\\frac{49}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561516 :question/problem "![](images/0d/0de50141c687bd36fc592bfdaa0d755c8838da9a81c81188ebfc6b408320405b.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(1,5)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=\\frac{9}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-5)^{2} & =\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)^{2} \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-5)^{2} & =\\frac{81}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561531 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨3 y^{3} - \\frac{y}{8},\\,9 x y^{2} + \\frac{7 x}{8}⟩\n$$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=⟨3 y e^{3 x},\\,x - 3 e^{3 x}⟩$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{8} + 3 e^{3 x + 3 y},\\,\\frac{9 x}{8} + 3 e^{3 x + 3 y}⟩\n$$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(9 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)+\\left(9 y^{2}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 y^{2}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3 e^{3 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 9 e^{3 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 9 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 9 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x}\\right)=1 - 12 e^{3 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III gives the required area. Notice that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{1}{8}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{9}{8},\n$$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{9}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{9}{8}\\right)+\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561533 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨3 y^{3} - \\frac{y}{8},\\,9 x y^{2} + \\frac{7 x}{8}⟩\n$$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=⟨3 y e^{2 x},\\,x - 3 e^{2 x}⟩$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{8} + 2 e^{2 x + 2 y},\\,\\frac{9 x}{8} + 2 e^{2 x + 2 y}⟩\n$$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(9 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)+\\left(9 y^{2}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 y^{2}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3 e^{2 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 6 e^{2 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 6 e^{2 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{2 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 6 e^{2 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{2 x}\\right)=1 - 9 e^{2 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III gives the required area. Notice that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4 e^{2 x + 2 y} + \\frac{1}{8}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4 e^{2 x + 2 y} + \\frac{9}{8},\n$$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(4 e^{2 x + 2 y} + \\frac{9}{8}\\right)-\\left(4 e^{2 x + 2 y} + \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{9}{8}\\right)+\\left(4 e^{2 x + 2 y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(4 e^{2 x + 2 y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561538 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{6} + 3 e^{3 x + 2 y},\\,\\frac{7 x}{6} + 2 e^{3 x + 2 y}⟩\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨y^{2} - \\frac{y}{6},\\,2 x y + \\frac{5 x}{6}⟩\n$$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨y e^{3 x},\\,x - e^{3 x}⟩$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6 e^{3 x + 2 y} + \\frac{1}{6}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6 e^{3 x + 2 y} + \\frac{7}{6},\n$$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(6 e^{3 x + 2 y} + \\frac{7}{6}\\right)-\\left(6 e^{3 x + 2 y} + \\frac{1}{6}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{7}{6}\\right)+\\left(6 e^{3 x + 2 y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)-\\left(6 e^{3 x + 2 y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2 y - \\frac{1}{6}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2 y + \\frac{5}{6},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(2 y + \\frac{5}{6}\\right)-\\left(2 y - \\frac{1}{6}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)+\\left(2 y\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{6}\\right)-\\left(2 y\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=e^{3 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 3 e^{3 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 3 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(e^{3 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 3 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(e^{3 x}\\right)=1 - 4 e^{3 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561545 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{8} + 3 e^{3 x + y},\\,\\frac{9 x}{8} + e^{3 x + y}⟩\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨2 y^{3} - \\frac{y}{8},\\,6 x y^{2} + \\frac{7 x}{8}⟩\n$$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨2 y e^{3 x},\\,x - 2 e^{3 x}⟩$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3 e^{3 x + y} + \\frac{1}{8}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3 e^{3 x + y} + \\frac{9}{8},\n$$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(3 e^{3 x + y} + \\frac{9}{8}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x + y} + \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{9}{8}\\right)+\\left(3 e^{3 x + y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x + y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(6 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8}\\right)-\\left(6 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)+\\left(6 y^{2}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(6 y^{2}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2 e^{3 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 6 e^{3 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 6 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(2 e^{3 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 6 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(2 e^{3 x}\\right)=1 - 8 e^{3 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561555 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨3 y^{2} - \\frac{y}{4},\\,6 x y + \\frac{3 x}{4}⟩\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{4} + 3 e^{3 x + 3 y},\\,\\frac{5 x}{4} + 3 e^{3 x + 3 y}⟩\n$$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨3 y e^{3 x},\\,x - 3 e^{3 x}⟩$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6 y - \\frac{1}{4}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6 y + \\frac{3}{4},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(6 y + \\frac{3}{4}\\right)-\\left(6 y - \\frac{1}{4}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)+\\left(6 y\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{4}\\right)-\\left(6 y\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II gives the required area. Notice that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{1}{4}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{5}{4},\n$$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{5}{4}\\right)-\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{1}{4}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)+\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3 e^{3 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 9 e^{3 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 9 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 9 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x}\\right)=1 - 12 e^{3 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562396 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-u+v &=-1 \\\\ 3u+3v &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(u,v)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$(u,v)=($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the first equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $v$ by adding $u$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\nv=u-1\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $v$ with $u-1$ in the second equation and solve for $u:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3u+3v & =5 \\\\ 3u+3(u-1) & =5 \\\\ 3u+3u-3 & =5 \\\\ 6u & =8 \\\\ u & =\\frac{8}{6} \\\\ u & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $u=\\frac{4}{3}$ into $v=u-1,$ then we can find the value of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u-1 \\\\ v & =\\frac{4}{3}-1 \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=\\frac{4}{3},\\,v=\\frac{1}{3},$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{4}{3},\\frac{1}{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562595 :question/problem "Given that $f(r,\\theta)=- r^{2} (\\cos {4 \\theta} - 2)$, find $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂r}\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta}\\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r}=- 2 r (\\cos {4 \\theta} - 2),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta}=4 r^{2} \\sin {4 \\theta}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(- 2 r (\\cos {4 \\theta} - 2))\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}(4 r^{2} \\sin {4 \\theta})\\sin \\theta \\\\ & =4r\\cos \\theta-2r\\cos 4\\theta\\cos \\theta-4r\\sin 4\\theta\\sin \\theta \\\\ & =-r(-4\\cos \\theta+2\\cos 4\\theta\\cos \\theta+4\\sin 4\\theta\\sin \\theta).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
